---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Dental practice with 3 locations in a bilingual Italian-German territory, Northern Italy' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Advertising management since 2025' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Google Ads and Meta Ads strategy and management, copywriting, bilingual communication setup' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Google Ads Search and Local (primary), Meta Ads' },
  { label: 'Team', value: 'Practice owners (German adaptation), social media manager' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '−53%', label: 'cost per lead, same format 2025 → 2026' },
  { metric: '4×', label: 'bilingual structure efficiency vs monolingual' },
  { metric: '+40%', label: 'revenue 2024 → 2026, figure shared by the practice' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  metaTitle="Local marketing in a bilingual territory"
  title="Local marketing in a bilingual territory: three locations, two languages, one system"
  description="Case study: bilingual Italian-German advertising for a multi-location practice. Google Ads Search and Local, Meta Ads, cost per lead halved in a year."
  eyebrow="Google Ads · Meta Ads · Bilingual communication"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2 class="fade-in">The context</h2>
  <p>
    Three locations spread across an area where language isn't a detail — it's a market factor.
    Part of the catchment searches for "dentista", the rest for "Zahnarzt". The practice runs
    officially in both languages, with dedicated email addresses and bilingual staff. But the
    three locations didn't start from the same place: one in particular struggled far more than
    the other two to bring in inquiries.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Build an advertising presence that works in two languages without doubling the cost or
    watering down the message — and rebalance demand across locations with wildly different
    potential.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">My role</h2>
  <p>
    Strategy and hands-on management of both channels since 2025. I wrote the Italian copy and
    built the strategic framework behind it; the German copy came out of working directly with
    the practice owners, who I walked through the logic behind each Italian version so the German
    result would land as a cultural adaptation, not a literal translation. To a native speaker,
    mechanically translated copy is recognizable in half a second.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    The underlying question: split the two audiences into separate campaign structures, or keep
    them together? The data answered clearly. The bilingual structure produced conversions at
    roughly a quarter of the cost of the campaign running solely for the German-speaking audience.
  </p>
  <p>
    So I consolidated everything into one structure and paused the separate one. It wasn't just
    about cost — two campaigns competing over the same territory scattered searches and muddied
    the results. One structure made performance steadier and easier to optimize.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worth flagging: "conversion" here isn't just a submitted form. It covers everything that
    actually matters for a multi-location practice — calls, website inquiries, direction requests,
    WhatsApp messages.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Execution</h2>
  <p>
    On Google Ads: bilingual Search coverage across all three locations, paired with local
    campaigns to catch proximity search. On Meta: lead-gen campaigns tied to recurring initiatives
    — an implantology open week, a prevention month, hygiene and whitening — with creative and
    targeting split by location, plus low-cost boosting of organic content (CPC between €0.09 and
    €0.32) to keep a steady presence between initiatives.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the underperforming location, we worked two fronts at once. With the social media manager,
    we built a dedicated storytelling project spotlighting the specialists there, the quality of
    care, the human side of the team. In parallel, I ran dedicated Meta lead-gen campaigns focused
    on the treatments with the best ratio of real demand to margin — instead of pushing a generic
    offer on a catchment that was already responding poorly.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    One detail that says more than most results: the campaign for that location took three tries
    to find the right configuration. The work is iterative. It's never a straight line.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Results</h2>
  <p>
    The most solid number is the improvement on the same format: the same implantology lead-gen
    campaign went from a cost per lead of €40.44 in May 2025 to €19.15 in April 2026 — roughly
    half — while lead volume nearly doubled, from 18 to 30. Altogether the recurring initiatives
    generated 58 leads on Meta.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the business side, the figures the practice shared in periodic reviews point to patient
    base growth of over 30% and revenue growth of over 40% between 2024 and 2026, with a real
    recovery in inquiries for the location that started furthest behind.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    A note on the source: unlike other projects, this client had no formal tracking through to
    revenue. The growth figures come from periodic reviews with the owners, not a structured
    measurement system — I'm reporting them as order-of-magnitude indications, not verified
    metrics.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    Bilingualism isn't a translation problem. It's a campaign architecture one — keeping the two
    audiences together turned out to be more efficient than splitting them, which ran against my
    first instinct. And when locations have different potential, the aggregate number hides more
    than it shows. Only by drilling down to the single location do you see where to act, and
    which lever to pull.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
